          "summary": "Step 3 (Route Ctrl\u002BZ/Ctrl\u002BY through the single authority) is fully supported by repository evidence. The commit is HEAD on feature/prompt-undo-across-regions, touches exactly the 4 host-recorded files (\u002B265/-3), and leaves tasks.md unmodified. Single-authority routing is real and non-bypassable: Window_PreviewKeyDown is wired at the Window root element (MainWindow.xaml line 15), the WPF tunneling stage that fires before the host RichTextBox and before any focused region\u0027s AvalonEdit; TryRoutePromptUndoRedo intercepts Ctrl\u002BZ / Ctrl\u002BY / Ctrl\u002BShift\u002BZ and sets e.Handled=true when PromptOwnsUndoContext() is true, so no native undo command binding fires. Native host undo is suppressed via PromptTextBox.IsUndoEnabled=false at startup, and the SetPlainText / fence-render helpers restore IsUndoEnabled only conditionally (if (undoWasEnabled)), so the disabled state persists; the case-cycle paths were changed to capture-and-restore. Each region\u0027s AvalonEdit undo is neutralized by _editor.Document.UndoStack.SizeLimit=0 in the AvalonEditRegionEditor constructor. The timeline has content: a baseline is captured at wiring time, host edits push via PromptTextBox_TextChanged (wired MainWindow.xaml line 3576) and in-region edits via the new EmbeddedCodeRegionBlock.AnyRegionContentEdited event into OnEmbeddedRegionContentEdited; the IsApplying re-entrancy guard in PushSnapshot/Apply prevents the restore feedback loop. In-region routing is covered because PromptOwnsUndoContext() includes IsInsideEmbeddedCodeRegion(Keyboard.FocusedElement). Coalescing/clear-on-send are legitimately deferred to Step 4 and the Clear() seam exists-but-uncalled, which is acceptable. The live keystroke outcome is a Step 6 human-observable fixture; all Step 3 structural requirements are established by inspection plus a green headless suite (244 passed).",
